// never polls the HID state, so it never pays for the probe.
final useTui =
    headlessPrompt == null && stdout.supportsAnsiEscapes && io.isInteractive;

Excellent — this resolves everything cleanly! In stream-json mode (and HEP events mode), the host wraps io in `HepEventsIO`, which **drops `io.write`** ("prose writes are dropped"). So `io.write('$line\n')`:

- Plain headless → stdout ✓ (ticket requirement)
- TUI → guarded by `!_useTui` ✓ (also, real TUI never runs runHeadless; and useTui is false when headlessPrompt != null)
- stream-json/HEP modes → `io.write` dropped by HepEventsIO → wire stays pure ✓ — **no extra flag or parameter needed!**

This is clearly the intended design: the codebase already has the "stdout-exclusivity rule" pattern. So implementation is simply:

```dart
final closed = ledger.segments.lastOrNull;
if (closed != null) {
  final line = usageTokensLogLine(sessionId: metadata.id, segment: closed);
  _logDiagnostic(line);
  // gh-1292: the diag file dies with an ephemeral CI runner (~/.fah is
  // gone when the leg ends), so the dmtools-agents reporter grepping the
  // run log found nothing. Mirror the line to stdout in non-TUI
  // sessions — [CliIO.write] is the stdout channel (headless hosts route
  // writeln to stderr); structured modes wrap io in HepEventsIO, which
  // drops write, so the HEP/stream-json wires stay pure.
  if (!_useTui) io.write('$line\n');
}
```

Wait — but should REPL line-mode /exit flushes also emit? With this unconditional `!_useTui` guard, yes: `/exit` in line REPL prints fa-tokens to stdout. Ticket: "headless mode (no TUI attached — the leg invocation)". Line REPL is interactive, not a leg invocation. Hmm. But the stated guard in the ticket is only TUI-vs-rest. Trade-off:

- If I gate on headless only, I need a flag/parameter since `_headlessMode` is cleared before flush.
- If I gate on `!_useTui`, line REPL exit also prints the line.

Which is more correct? The ticket explicitly frames the guard as "TUI sessions keep stdout clean; headless/CI sessions get the line". It doesn't demand line-REPL silence. Emitting on /exit in a line REPL is a minor cosmetic thing; and arguably useful (user sees token usage). Also simpler, no signature change. But is it "more production code than needed to pass the test" (TDD rule R2)? The tests I write: headless→stdout, TUI→clean, stream-json→pure. A `!_useTui` guard passes all three with the least machinery and matches the ticket's literal guard statement. I'll go with `!_useTui`.

Hmm, one more consideration — the diag log timestamp prefix: the fa.log line is `'${timestamp} $message'`. The stdout line is just the raw `fa-tokens: {...}` line — matches the reporter's pinned regex (usageTokensLogPattern matches `^fa-tokens: ...`) — good, actually BETTER for the reporter since no timestamp prefix.

Now, `io.write('$line\n')` — is there a risk the TUI `_TuiCliIO` delegates when `_tui == null` even in TUI sessions? Guard prevents reaching it in TUI sessions anyway.

