- If I gate on headless only, I need a flag/parameter since `_headlessMode` is cleared before flush.
- If I gate on `!_useTui`, line REPL exit also prints the line.

Which is more correct? The ticket explicitly frames the guard as "TUI sessions keep stdout clean; headless/CI sessions get the line". It doesn't demand line-REPL silence. Emitting on /exit in a line REPL is a minor cosmetic thing; and arguably useful (user sees token usage). Also simpler, no signature change. But is it "more production code than needed to pass the test" (TDD rule R2)? The tests I write: headless→stdout, TUI→clean, stream-json→pure. A `!_useTui` guard passes all three with the least machinery and matches the ticket's literal guard statement. I'll go with `!_useTui`.

